=== nms ===

NMS COMMANDS (NTC Music):
  nms play [-sf <dur>] <file>            Play sound in the terminal (Ctrl+C stops; -sf starts from an offset)
  nms scan [-d <n>] [-s] [-l <dur>] [--care]
    List audio + video files (dur: 25s, 1mn, 1h)
  nms scan -l <dur>                      Only list media longer than <dur> (e.g. 25s, 1mn, 1h)
  Plays in the terminal with a live position/remaining countdown · video plays audio only (through ffmpeg) · Ctrl+C stops
Examples:
    nms play -sf 30 song.mp3               # Start an audio file at 0:30
    nms play movie.mp4                     # Play a video's audio track
    nms scan -d 2 -s                       # List media with sizes
    nms scan -l 1mn                        # List media longer than 1 minute
=== nprintf ===

NPRINTF COMMAND:
  nprintf <format> [arguments...]        Bash-style formatted output
    CONVERSIONS
    %s text · %d/%i signed · %u unsigned · %o octal · %x/%X hex · %c first character
    %b escapes in the argument · %q shell-quoted · %f/%e/%g/%a floats · %% literal %
    FLAGS, WIDTH, PRECISION
    %-8s left · %05d zero-pad · %+d sign · %.2f precision · %*s width from an argument
    ESCAPES (a backslash starts each one)
    \n newline · \t tab · \r return · \\ backslash · \" quote · \xHH hex · \0NNN octal · \c stop output
    WHY \n?
    nprintf adds nothing after the format. End with \n when you want a line break; without it, output continues on the same line.
    QUOTING
    Wrap the format in double quotes so spaces and escapes stay in one argument.
    The format repeats while arguments remain; a missing %s is empty and a missing number is 0.
Examples:
    nprintf "hello %s\n" world             # One line; \n ends it
    nprintf "%s has %d items\n" cart 3     # Text and integer
    nprintf "%-8s|%05d|%.2f\n" left 42 3.14159 # Left, zero-pad, precision
    nprintf "%b\n" "a\tb"                  # %b applies escapes from the argument
    nprintf "no newline"                   # Stays on the same line
