=== pipe ===

PIPES & REDIRECTION [NON-COMMAND]:
  a | b                                  Send a's output to b as input
  a && b                                 Run b only if a succeeds
  a || b                                 Run b only if a fails
  a ; b                                  Run b even if a fails (spaces required)
  cmd < file                             Read input from file
  cmd > file                             Write output to file (overwrite)
  cmd >> file                            Append output to file
  cmd <<EOF ... EOF                      Read input until the EOF line
  cmd <<< text                           Read input from the literal text token
  cmd | <filter>                         Pipe filters: head, tail, betw, skip, gs, sort, rev, count, trim, encode, qur, clip (their flags complete too)
  Operators need spaces around them; use - to read stdin
  Hints: | lists the pipe filters; >> and < list text files only; > shows none; << only EOF
Examples:
    gs main > results.txt                  # Save search results to a file
    view readme.md | encode b64            # Encode a file's contents
    gs main | say                          # Page-search results through say
    head 20 app.log                        # First 20 lines of a file (or piped input)
    gs error | sort -u | head 10           # Unique sorted matches, first 10
    echo nice ... bro ... | trim " ..."    # Strip a pattern from piped text (inline: trim " ..." "nice ... bro ...")
=== py_activate ===

PYTHON COMMANDS:
  py_activate                            Activate Python virtual environment (auto-detect)
  py_activate <path>                     Activate specific venv path
Examples:
    py_activate                            # Auto-activate Python venv
    py_activate .venv                      # Activate specific venv
=== py_deactivate ===

PY_DEACTIVATE COMMAND:
  py_deactivate                          Deactivate current virtual environment
Examples:
    py_deactivate                          # Deactivate venv
=== qr ===

QR CODE COMMANDS:
  qr <text>                              Encode text and display QR in terminal
  qr encode <text> -o <file>             Save QR as PNG image
  qr encode <image> -o <file>            Encode an image into a QR (scan to view image)
  qr decode <image>                      Decode QR code from image
Examples:
    qr "Hello World"                       # Display QR code in terminal
    qr encode "Hello" -o hello.png         # Save QR as PNG
    qr encode image.png -o imageqrcode.png # Encode image so scanning shows it
    qr decode qr.png                       # Decode QR from image
